From b6b8984fbf091b455d1fdaf4ae7834cf8bc6a4fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Allen <cma@bitemyapp.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 22:39:02 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Animate view drawing in its presented bounds, and draw soft
 shadows faster

A view-backed layer's drawing showed at the rect it was drawn for, the
model's bounds, whatever bounds the layer presented. While an animation
changed a view's frame (through animator), its new drawing showed whole
from the animated origin: a sliver meant to sweep a segment showed full
width at once, past the top of what held it, and since frame damage
follows the presented bounds, what it drew outside them stayed on
screen as a trail. A canvas now shows in the presented bounds, placed by
the layer's gravity as an image of the bounds it was drawn for would be,
so it resizes with the layer as AppKit's drawing does.

A group's shadow was worked out over its whole clip (a window's worth
for a composited layer tree) though it reaches only as far as what the
group draws, moved by the offset and spread by the blur; that is all it
covers now, inside the clip, here and when sizing an enclosing group.
The coverage of a group's shadow is its layer's alpha, taken as it is
rather than drawn again. The box blurs run down columns a row at a time,
with each mean an exact multiply rather than a division. An image whose
pixels land one to one on whole device pixels, as a view's drawing
composited where it was drawn does, is sampled without filtering. Each
change leaves the same pixels: switching an application's surfaces, which
redrew a window with a lifted card in it, went from about 200 ms of
rasterizing to about 25.

The null render thread can show windows at a scale other than 1
(testing::use_null_backend_scale), so captured pixels test 2x drawing;
testing::request_composite_at asks for a frame without waiting, for code
that can't run the loop. A test drives a segmented switch's press and
release at 1x and 2x and fails on any trail; others check that a group's
shadow doesn't depend on its clip and costs what it covers, and that the
blur is the exact mean. SIDESTEP_TRACE_FRAMES=tiles also reports the
window surface's damage and the slowest operations of slow paints.

Signed-off-by: Chris Allen <cma@bitemyapp.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
